  "step_2": "The individual reports choosing Lottery A because they perceived 3000 Euros with 90% probability as a better deal. They emphasize that even in the worst case (getting 0), there are no deductions, which makes it a good choice. They contrast this with Lottery B, which they describe as having 'no such high probability of winning'. The individual focuses on the high probability (90%) of a positive outcome in A and the relatively low probability (45%) of winning in B. Their justification centers on the likelihood of receiving a positive payoff and the attractiveness of a high-probability, moderate reward. There is no mention of skewness, distributional shape, or unfavorable outcomes being more probable as a reason for preference. The deliberation is based on expected utility-like considerations, particularly the high chance of gain in A.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on which lottery is more right-skewed, i.e., which assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes, and prefers the more right-skewed one. In this case, Lottery B is more right-skewed (55% on 0) than A (10% on 0), so the reason would favor B. However, the individual chose A and justified this by emphasizing the high probability of a favorable outcome in A, which is the opposite of focusing on unfavorable outcomes. The individual values the low risk of loss in A, while the decision reason would value the higher risk (more right-skew) in B. There is no alignment in either the information considered (the individual ignores skewness and focuses on win probability) or the decision rule (preferring safety vs. preferring greater skewness in losses). Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
