h lotteries involve losses, so 'favorable' outcomes are less negative. In this context, a right-skewed lottery would have a small chance of a very bad loss and high chance of a small loss — which is not the case. A is 50% -1000, 50% 0 — this is not right-skewed. B is certain -500. So no lottery is right-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason cannot be clearly applied — it may not yield a preference. However, for the sake of analysis, we proceed assuming the reason can assess skewness in loss domains and prefers more probability on worse outcomes (i.e., more right-skewed in loss space). But this contradicts typical definitions. So Step 1 concludes: the decision reason is poorly applicable due to the nature of the lotteries (loss domain, certainty), but if forced, it would not prefer A as more right-skewed — if anything, A is left-skewed in value. So the reason likely does not favor A. But the verbal report chooses B, so if the reason prefers B, it must be because B is more right-skewed — but B is certain, so not skewed. Thus, the reason cannot clearly be applied. However, we proceed to Step 2.",
