  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on skewness — specifically preferring more right-skewed lotteries (with higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). However, the individual does not consider skewness or distribution shape at all. Instead, they reinterpret the problem by introducing an external gain (2000 Euros) and evaluate the options in terms of percentage losses relative to that gain and the resulting net outcome. Their justification centers on the certainty of a positive net result (1500) with B, which they prefer over the risk in A. This is consistent with risk aversion or certainty effect, not skewness preference. Moreover, the lotteries involve only losses or zero, so 'right-skewed' in the usual sense (long tail of high gains) does not apply. The individual’s reasoning is based on net final wealth and certainty, not on the probabilistic structure of the outcomes. There is no overlap between the information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) and that considered by the individual (net gain, percentage loss, certainty). Similarly, the justification (guaranteed profit) does not align with the rule of preferring more right-skewed distributions. In fact, if anything, B is not skewed at all, so it cannot be preferred on skewness grounds. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used the skewness-based reason. The reasoning is entirely different in nature.",
